  "lean": {
    "statement": "\n    {X Y : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] [MeasurableSpace Y]\n    (μ : Measure X) (ν : Measure Y) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]\n    (F : X × Y → ℝ) (hF : Measurable F)\n    (hF0 : ∀ z, 0 ≤ F z) (hFb : ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ z, F z ≤ C) :\n    let Φ : ℝ → ℝ := fun t => t * Real.log t\n    let A : X → ℝ := fun x => ∫ y, F (x, y) ∂ν\n    let B : Y → ℝ := fun y => ∫ x, F (x, y) ∂μ\n    let m : ℝ := ∫ z, F z ∂μ.prod ν\n    Integrable F (μ.prod ν) ∧\n    Integrable (fun z => Φ (F z)) (μ.prod ν) ∧\n    (∀ x, Integrable (fun y => F (x, y)) ν ∧\n      Integrable (fun y => Φ (F (x, y))) ν) ∧\n    (∀ y, Integrable (fun x => F (x, y)) μ ∧\n      Integrable (fun x => Φ (F (x, y))) μ) ∧\n    Integrable A μ ∧ Integrable B ν ∧\n    Integrable (fun x => Φ (A x)) μ ∧ Integrable (fun y => Φ (B y)) ν ∧\n    (∫ x, Φ (A x) ∂μ) + (∫ y, Φ (B y) ∂ν) ≤\n      (∫ z, Φ (F z) ∂μ.prod ν) + Φ m",
    "statement_sha256": "fcb2124a5d87baca80ba65088cfd652a757d15b2ef5ff07cb5512f32dccdc090",
    "compiled": true,
    "approved_definition_context": [
      "X and Y are arbitrary measurable spaces. mu and nu are actual probability measures; their product is the actual product measure. All integrals are real Bochner integrals.",
      "F is a real-valued measurable function on the product, nonnegative at every point and bounded above by some real constant. There is no positive lower bound or positive total mass premise.",
      "Phi(t)=t*Real.log(t), with the totalized real logarithm including zero. A(x) and B(y) are the actual displayed slice integrals, and m is the actual full product integral.",
      "Integrable means genuine Bochner integrability of the displayed function under its stated measure. Every slice domain is quantified at all x or all y, not only almost everywhere.",
      "The conclusion supplies joint, all-slice and both marginal integrability for the function and Phi, and the literal inequality J_A+J_B<=J_F+Phi(m). No normalized relative-entropy, logarithmic Sobolev or transport conclusion is part of the statement."
    ]
  },
